<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Hong Kong Polytechnic University
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Hong Kong, China 
<br class="ltx_break">kelvin.so@cpce-polyu.edu.hk
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yueyue Sun<sup id="id3" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>, Jun-Min Wang<sup id="id4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">School of Mathematics and Statistics</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Beijing Institute of Technology
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Beijing, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id7" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>yysunnya@bit.edu.cn, <sup id="id8" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>jmwang@bit.edu.cn
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Siu Pang Yung
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Department of Mathematics</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The University of Hong Kong
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Hong Kong, China 
<br class="ltx_break">spyung@hku.hk
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Anthony Wai Keung Loh<sup id="id11" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>, Chun Pong Chau<sup id="id12" class="ltx_sup">4</sup>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">School of Professional Education and Executive Development</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Hong Kong Polytechnic University
<br class="ltx_break"></span>Hong Kong, China 
<br class="ltx_break"><sup id="id15" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>anthony.wk.loh@cpce-polyu.edu.hk, <sup id="id16" class="ltx_sup">4</sup>james.chau@cpce-polyu.edu.hk
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">How far are Large Language Models (LLMs) in performing deep relational reasoning? In this paper, we evaluate and compare the reasoning capabilities of three cutting-edge LLMs, namely, DeepSeek-R1, DeepSeek-V3 and GPT-4o, through a suite of carefully designed benchmark tasks in family tree and general graph reasoning. Our experiments reveal that DeepSeek-R1 consistently achieves the highest F1-scores across multiple tasks and problem sizes, demonstrating strong aptitude in logical deduction and relational inference. However, all evaluated models, including DeepSeek-R1, struggle significantly as problem complexity increases, largely due to token length limitations and incomplete output structures. A detailed analysis of DeepSeek-R1’s long Chain-of-Thought responses uncovers its unique planning and verification strategies, but also highlights instances of incoherent or incomplete reasoning, calling attention to the need for deeper scrutiny into LLMs’ internal inference dynamics.
We further discuss key directions for future work, including the role of multimodal reasoning and the systematic examination of reasoning failures. Our findings provide both empirical insights and theoretical implications for advancing LLMs’ reasoning abilities, particularly in tasks that demand structured, multi-step logical inference.
Our code repository will be publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/kelvinhkcs/Deep-Relational-Reasoning" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/kelvinhkcs/Deep-Relational-Reasoning</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Large Language Models (LLMs), Relational Reasoning, Deep Reasoning, DeepSeek, Chain-of-Thought (CoT)

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across a broad spectrum of tasks, such as time-series analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> and AI-driven education <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. In particular, the recent release of DeepSeek-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, the first LLM equipped with deep reasoning capabilities, has spurred growing optimism that LLMs can now perform complex logical reasoning at a level potentially surpassing human proficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">With the rise of LLMs, increasing attention has been directed toward understanding their reasoning abilities, widely considered a critical bottleneck in the pursuit of Artificial General Intelligence (AGI) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Prior studies have primarily evaluated reasoning performance through benchmarks involving arithmetic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and symbolic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> etc. While many LLMs developed in recent years <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> have achieved impressive results on these benchmarks, they largely lack explicit deep reasoning mechanisms. Preliminary work has begun to explore the reasoning abilities of more recent LLMs with deep reasoning capabilities, such as DeepSeek-R1, in some reasoning domains, for example, arithmetic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. However, a comprehensive understanding of their general logical reasoning capacity remains underexplored.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Concurrently, there has been ongoing debate about whether the existing reasoning benchmarks are truly indicative of deep reasoning ability. Critics argue that many of these benchmarks require only shallow or pattern-based reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>, making them insufficient for validating the sophisticated reasoning capabilities of modern LLMs. One key area that warrants deeper investigation is relational reasoning, which is the ability to understand and reason about relationships between entities. This faculty is a cornerstone of intelligence, closely tied to logical thinking and problem-solving in unfamiliar contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Recent research has also suggested that relational reasoning may remain a fundamental weakness of current LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Motivated by these gaps, this paper investigates the relational reasoning abilities of LLMs with deep reasoning capabilities, particularly DeepSeek-R1, by comparing their performance against other widely-used LLMs. Our study aims to:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluate the relational reasoning performance of state-of-the-art LLMs with deep reasoning capabilities, with a focus on DeepSeek-R1;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Explore the limitations of these models in complex relational problems, particularly in scenarios that remain underexamined in existing literature;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Derive actionable insights to inform future LLM development and research directions.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To conduct a thorough evaluation, we propose two classes of relational reasoning benchmarks: family tree and general graph reasoning, using a zero-shot prompting setup. Our family tree benchmark involves identifying four distinct family tree relations - sister, aunt, grandson, and paternal great aunt - within large and complex family structures, while our general graph reasoning benchmark include node connectivity and shortest-path problems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Our experimental findings reveal several key insights:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs with deep reasoning capabilities, such as DeepSeek-R1, significantly outperform prior models without such capabilities in deep relational reasoning problems.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary bottleneck in these models lies in the token limit inherent to current LLM architectures, which restricts their capacity to handle large relational structures.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While DeepSeek-R1 demonstrates strong planning, verification, and step-by-step deduction abilities, some intermediate steps are unstructured, suggesting that, despite appearing coherent, the underlying reasoning rationale may not always be fully sound.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">This paper offers the following key contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To the best of our knowledge, this is the first comprehensive evaluation of relational reasoning in state-of-the-art LLMs with deep reasoning capabilities, with a particular focus on DeepSeek-R1.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce and apply an experimental pipeline of two representative benchmarks, family tree and general graph reasoning, designed to systematically evaluate LLMs’ relational reasoning capacity in a zero-shot prompting manner.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We demonstrate that DeepSeek-R1, known for its long Chain-of-Thought reasoning enabled by reinforcement learning, fundamentally outperforms other LLMs (e.g., GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3) in relational reasoning problems.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of the paper is organized as follows: Section <a href="#S2" title="II Related Works ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> reviews related work. Section <a href="#S3" title="III Relational Reasoning Benchmarks ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> introduces our two benchmarks: family tree and general graph reasoning. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Experiments ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> details our experimental setup, including the models under evaluation, the data generation processes, and the procedure and metrics of evaluation. Section <a href="#S5" title="V Result Analysis and Discussion ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> presents our experimental results and an in-depth discussion. Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Conclusion ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> concludes the paper. Our code repository will be publicly available at <a href="https://github.com/kelvinhkcs/Deep-Relational-Reasoning" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">https://github.com/kelvinhkcs/Deep-Relational-Reasoning</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Works</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Large Language Models</span></h3>

<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Large Language Models</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models (LLMs) refer to generative, pre-trained, Transformer-based AI models. By leveraging massive corpora of data representing human knowledge, along with advanced pre-training and finetuning techniques, LLMs have demonstrated unprecedented, human-like reasoning capabilities and have achieved state-of-the-art performance in problems once considered infeasible, such as machine translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, music composition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, and more. These models are typically trained using an autoregressive loss function to maximize the likelihood of predicting the next token given the preceding context <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal LLMs</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike standard LLMs that process only textual input, multimodal LLMs are capable of understanding and generating responses from diverse input types, such as images, audio, and video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. A well-known example is Contrastive Language–Image Pre-training (CLIP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. These models typically employ a unified embedding mechanism that enables the joint processing of multiple modalities by embedding and concatenating their token representations into a single sequence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Logical Reasoning of LLMs</h5>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With the rapid evolution of LLMs, an increasing body of research has focused on their logical reasoning abilities, particularly in domains such as arithmetic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite> and symbolic reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. While earlier LLMs have demonstrated strong performance on such benchmarks, recent studies reveal that models lacking explicit deep reasoning capabilities still struggle with problems requiring structured decision-making, such as the “blocksworld” problem, which involves arranging blocks in a specific order <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Moreover, despite the emergence of more powerful models with purported deep reasoning abilities, comprehensive evaluation of their performance on general logical reasoning problems remains limited and urgently needed.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Prompting Techniques</span></h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Few-shot Prompting</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Few-shot prompting involves providing LLMs with a few guiding examples within the prompt to help the model better understand the task and generate more coherent and structured outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. This foundational strategy has inspired several prompt engineering variants, including Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, Chain-of-Verification (CoVe) prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and Tree-of-Thought (ToT) prompting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. These approaches aim to introduce intermediate reasoning steps or verification structures that guide the model through more rigorous logical sequences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Zero-shot Prompting</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Zero-shot prompting refers to querying LLMs without providing any explicit problems examples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>. This approach relies solely on the model’s pre-trained knowledge and reasoning capabilities, offering flexibility in unseen problems. However, it often comes with trade-offs in performance and robustness, especially for problems requiring structured, multi-step reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal Prompting</h5>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal prompting involves querying multimodal LLMs with inputs spanning multiple modalities - text, images, audio, or video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. This technique enables the model to engage in more holistic forms of reasoning, such as spatial, visual, and geometric reasoning. Recent studies suggest that multimodal prompting enhances reasoning robustness and generalization by mimicking the diverse ways in which humans process and interpret information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Relational Reasoning Benchmarks</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we introduce the two relational reasoning benchmarks used in our study: family tree and general graph reasoning. These benchmarks are widely adopted in relational reasoning literature [32]–[36], especially for evaluating the capabilities of neural network-based models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Family Tree Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The family tree reasoning benchmark consists of problems that require the deduction of complex family relationships based on a set of basic relations. Specifically, a family tree is constructed using four fundamental predicates:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsMother<math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: True if <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the mother of <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsFather<math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: True if <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the father of <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsSon<math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: True if <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the son of <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsDaughter<math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: True if <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the daughter of <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These basic relations are used to infer four higher-order target relations as described below:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HasSister<math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:
Determines whether <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has a sister.
Formally, this is evaluated as:
<math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\&gt;y\not=x,\exists\&gt;z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">∃</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">∃</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\&gt;y\not=x,\exists\&gt;z</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,y)\wedge\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,y)\wedge\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,x)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsGrandson<math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:
Determines whether <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the grandson of <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, this is evaluated as:
<math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\&gt;z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">∃</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\&gt;z</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsSon}(z,y)\wedge(\texttt{IsSon}(x,z)\vee\texttt{IsDaughter}(x,z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsSon</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsSon</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsSon}(z,y)\wedge(\texttt{IsSon}(x,z)\vee\texttt{IsDaughter}(x,z))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsAunt<math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:
Determines whether <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the aunt of <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, this is evaluated as:
<math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\&gt;z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">∃</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\&gt;z</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,y)\wedge\texttt{IsGrandparent}(x,z)\wedge\neg\texttt{IsMother}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsGrandparent</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">¬</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsMother</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,y)\wedge\texttt{IsGrandparent}(x,z)\wedge\neg\texttt{IsMother}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsGrandparent}(x,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsGrandparent</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsGrandparent}(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math> is evaluated by checking if <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\&gt;w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">∃</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\&gt;w</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="((\texttt{IsSon}(z,w)\wedge\texttt{IsFather}(x,w))\vee(\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,w)\wedge\texttt{IsMother}(x,w)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsSon</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsFather</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsMother</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">((\texttt{IsSon}(z,w)\wedge\texttt{IsFather}(x,w))\vee(\texttt{IsDaughter}(z,w)\wedge\texttt{IsMother}(x,w)))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsPaternalGreatAunt<math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:
Determines whether <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> is the paternal great aunt of <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally, this is evaluated as:
<math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\&gt;z,w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.387em">∃</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\&gt;z,w</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsGrandfather}(x,z)\wedge\texttt{IsParent}(z,w)\wedge\texttt{IsDaughter}(w,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsGrandfather</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsParent</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsGrandfather}(x,z)\wedge\texttt{IsParent}(z,w)\wedge\texttt{IsDaughter}(w,y)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsGrandfather}(x,z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsGrandfather</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsGrandfather}(x,z)</annotation></semantics></math> holds if <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exists\;v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">∃</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exists\;v</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\texttt{IsFather}(v,z)\wedge(\texttt{IsSon}(v,x)\vee\texttt{IsDaughter}(v,x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsFather</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsSon</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∨</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">IsDaughter</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\texttt{IsFather}(v,z)\wedge(\texttt{IsSon}(v,x)\vee\texttt{IsDaughter}(v,x))</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Other family relations, for example, <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsUncle<math id="S3.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S3.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsGrandparent<math id="S3.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>), can be derived similarly. However, due to logical symmetry and comparable reasoning depth, we adopt the four relations above as representative problems for our family tree reasoning benchmark.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">General Graph Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The general graph reasoning benchmark involves problems that require inferring high-level structural properties of graphs from a basic relation:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HasEdge<math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: True if there is a directed edge from node <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to node <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">From this base relation, we define two key target problems:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Connectivity<math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:
Determines whether a path exists from node <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to node <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Shortest<math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>:
Determines the number of edges on the shortest path from node <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to node <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">While both problems can be solved using classical graph algorithms, for example, Breadth-First Search (BFS) and Dijkstra’s algorithms, our objective is not to test algorithmic knowledge. Instead, we prompt LLMs with natural language descriptions (see Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Data Generation ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>)) to assess their relational reasoning abilities, rather than their ability to mimic algorithmic behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Other simpler problems in the general graph reasoning domain include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">4-Connectivity<math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: Determines if there is a path from node <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> to node <math id="S3.I5.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> within four edges.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">2-Outdegree<math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>: Determines if node <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> has exactly two outgoing edges.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">These problems are relatively straightforward, as they only require local neighborhood examination and do not challenge the model’s ability to conduct deep, multi-step reasoning across the entire graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we focus exclusively on the problems of <span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Connectivity<math id="S3.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Shortest<math id="S3.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> as they necessitate a more sophisticated, global understanding of the graph structure, mirroring the complexity found in our family tree reasoning benchmark problems. This allows us to conduct a more comprehensive and meaningful evaluation of the relational reasoning capabilities of LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section outlines the LLMs evaluated in our study, followed by our data generation process, our experimental setup and the evaluation metrics adopted.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Models</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively assess deep relational reasoning capabilities, we select DeepSeek-R1 as our primary model due to its demonstrated strengths in complex reasoning and its accessibility for large-scale testing. For meaningful comparative analysis, we also include DeepSeek-V3 and GPT-4o to represent models without deep reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>1 </span>DeepSeek-R1 (671B parameters)</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Released in early 2025, DeepSeek-R1 is a state-of-the-art LLM comprising 671 billion parameters. It is widely recognized for its remarkable reasoning performance and cost-efficiency, achieved through large-scale reinforcement learning. Notably, it is the first LLM to integrate several novel techniques, including Mixture of Experts (MoE) and Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO), to simultaneously improve reasoning and computational efficiency. The model is specifically trained to construct long Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning paths, which are vital for solving problems requiring complex logical deduction across extended contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>2 </span>DeepSeek-V3</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DeepSeek-V3 is a smaller and more lightweight version of DeepSeek-R1. While it omits full implementations of MoE and long CoT frameworks, it retains core advancements in reinforcement learning and structured reasoning. It serves as a representative model without full deep reasoning capabilities, allowing for fair performance comparisons.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span>3 </span>GPT-4o</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-4o, a leading model in the GPT series, offers competitive reasoning capabilities and long-context understanding. However, it does not adopt the explicit long CoT methodology employed in DeepSeek-R1. It is included as a strong benchmark model without a dedicated deep reasoning architecture.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Data Generation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The data generation process consists of three primary steps: 1) Adjacency and Boolean Matrix Construction, 2) Deduction of Target Relations, and 3) Translation into Natural Language Prompts.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span>1 </span>Adjacency and Boolean Matrix Construction</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Family Tree Reasoning: We generate four <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> Boolean matrices to represent the basic family relations: <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsFather<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsMother<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsSon<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsDaughter<math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. For instance, if the entry at <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> in the father matrix is 1, it implies person <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> is person <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s father.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">General Graph Reasoning: We randomly generate a <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\times n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\times n</annotation></semantics></math> adjacency matrix to represent a directed graph of <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> nodes. If there is a directed edge from node <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> to node <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, the entry at <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 1; otherwise, it is 0.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All generated data are verified to ensure logical consistency, particularly for family relationships. Our implementation is adapted from the benchmarks provided in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An illustrative example of the Boolean matrices for the family tree reasoning benchmark with <math id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> is shown below:</p>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.2" class="ltx_logical-block">
            
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="718.83" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 718.83" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,718.83) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.53 L 0 714.3 C 0 716.8 2.03 718.83 4.53 718.83 L 472.85 718.83 C 475.35 718.83 477.38 716.8 477.38 714.3 L 477.38 4.53 C 477.38 2.03 475.35 0 472.85 0 L 4.53 0 C 2.03 0 0 2.03 0 4.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 4.53 L 1.57 695.5 L 475.8 695.5 L 475.8 4.53 C 475.8 2.9 474.48 1.57 472.85 1.57 L 4.53 1.57 C 2.9 1.57 1.57 2.9 1.57 4.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 703.71)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="450.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.57em;">
