</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Result Analysis and Discussion</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we analyze the experimental results, examine their implications in depth, and outline future directions. The average F1-scores of the LLMs across six family tree and general graph reasoning benchmark problems, evaluated over 100 runs, are summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ IV-D Evaluation Metrics ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Family Tree Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all values of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40</annotation></semantics></math>, DeepSeek-R1 consistently achieves the highest average F1-scores, except at <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math> where all three evaluated models fail completely on the problems <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsGrandson<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsAunt<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsPaternalGreatAunt<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, each yielding an F1-score of 0.000. Also, for problems such as <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HasSister<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsGrandson<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsAunt<math id="S5.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, DeepSeek–R1 reaches impressive average F1-scores as high as 0.9 at both <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>, providing strong evidence of its advanced capability in deep relational reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, performance of DeepSeek-R1 on <span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsPaternalGreatAunt<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> remains relatively modest even at smaller scales (<math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> and 20), with F1-scores only slightly above 0.3. This likely reflects the higher logical complexity involved in deducing the paternal great aunt relation compared to other family relations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Among all problems, <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HasSister<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the least challenging, which explains why all three models achieve their highest scores on this problem (all above 0.5). This further supports the notion that DeepSeek-R1’s performance edge becomes more pronounced as relational complexity increases.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The performances of GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3 are generally comparable across all problems. While DeepSeek-V3 slightly outperforms GPT-4o in certain cases, the differences are not statistically significant. As both models lack specialized deep reasoning mechanisms, these findings indirectly affirm the superiority of DeepSeek-R1 in family tree reasoning problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">General Graph Reasoning</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Similar trends are observed in the general graph reasoning problems. At <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> and 20, DeepSeek-R1 consistently outperforms both GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3 on textttConnectivity<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Shortest<math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, achieving average F1-scores above 0.7 at <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> and above 0.4 at <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=20</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, both GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3 demonstrate only moderate performance, with DeepSeek-V3 showing a slight edge in certain runs. However, when <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math>, all three LLMs experience significant performance degradation, with average F1-scores dropping below 0.2. In some cases, such as with GPT-4o, the F1-scores even fall to zero, reflecting a complete failure. This mirrors the models’ struggles with more complex general graph reasoning problems, highlighting a shared limitation when scaling to deeper relational depths.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these results highlight DeepSeek-R1 as the most competent LLM for both types of relational reasoning, offering a marked performance advantage over models lacking deep reasoning capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In-depth Discussion</span></h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Intrinsic Token Limits</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All LLMs inherently face token limits dictated by their architecture. This constraint appears to be a key factor behind DeepSeek-R1’s failure at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math> across most problems except <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">HasSister<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>. Closer inspection of failed runs reveals instances where the reasoning process is truncated, and the output matrix is either incomplete or malformed. In our evaluation protocol, any such output is assigned an F1-score of 0, which penalizes performance in evaluation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The same limitation likely contributes to the performance breakdowns of GPT-4o and DeepSeek-V3 at higher <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> values. As prompt length and complexity grow with <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, these models are unable to complete their reasoning process and reply within the allowed token budget.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Long Chain-of-Thought</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DeepSeek-R1’s exceptional performance can be attributed in part to its long Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning, a core component of its architecture. Careful examination of its output reveals rich, structured reasoning not found in GPT-4o or DeepSeek-V3 responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Below, we present an illustrative excerpt from DeepSeek-R1’s response to an <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IsGrandson<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math></span> problem (described in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Data Generation ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Are Large Language Models Capable of Deep Relational Reasoning? Insights from DeepSeek-R1 and Benchmark Comparisons" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>). For clarity, we highlight several distinct reasoning behaviors using color-coded annotations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="1989.75" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 1989.75" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,1989.75) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.53 L 0 1985.23 C 0 1987.73 2.03 1989.75 4.53 1989.75 L 472.85 1989.75 C 475.35 1989.75 477.38 1987.73 477.38 1985.23 L 477.38 4.53 C 477.38 2.03 475.35 0 472.85 0 L 4.53 0 C 2.03 0 0 2.03 0 4.53 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.57 4.53 L 1.57 1966.43 L 475.8 1966.43 L 475.8 4.53 C 475.8 2.9 474.48 1.57 472.85 1.57 L 4.53 1.57 C 2.9 1.57 1.57 2.9 1.57 4.53 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 1974.63)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.57em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="450.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.57em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Example - Deep Reasoning Content of DeepSeek-R1</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 13.39 9.19)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:32.57em;--ltx-fo-height:141.1em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="1955.04" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 1952.35)" width="450.68"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:32.57em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">DeepSeek-R1: <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>think<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Okay, let’s try to figure out who the grandsons are in this family tree. First, I need to parse all the given relations and build a structure to represent the family members and their connections. Let’s start by listing out all the given facts one by one and see how they connect.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">The family members are P0 to P9. The relations given are:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">1. P4 is P1’s son.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.5" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">2. P2 is P5’s father.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.6" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">3. P1 is P9’s mother.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.7" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Omitted by the authors<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>…</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.8" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">24. P1 is P4’s mother.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.9" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Hmm, some of these might be redundant or reinforcing existing relationships. Let me start by mapping out each person’s parents and children.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.10" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Starting with P0. From fact 11, P0 is P4’s father. Fact 18 says P0 is P9’s father. Fact 17 says P9 is P0’s daughter. So P0 has children P4 (son) and P9 (daughter).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.11" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">P1: Fact 3 says P1 is P9’s mother. Fact 12 clarifies that P9 is P1’s daughter. Fact 24 says P1 is P4’s mother. So P1’s children are P4 (son) and P9 (daughter). But wait, fact 1 says P4 is P1’s son, which aligns with that. Also, P9 is their daughter.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.12" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.12.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">…<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>Content omitted for more compact presentation<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;" mathcolor="#0000FF">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>…</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">P9: Her parents are P0 (father) and P1 (mother) from facts 17,18 and 3,12. Her children are P3 (son), P5 (daughter?), wait no. Let’s check. Fact 7 says P3 is P9’s son. Fact 8: P8 is P9’s son. Fact 15: P5 is P9’s daughter. Fact 20: P7 is P9’s son. Wait, that’s conflicting. Let me check again.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.14.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">Wait, fact 6: P9 is P3’s mother. Fact 7: P3 is P9’s son. So P3 is a child of P9. Fact 8: P8 is P9’s son. Fact 13: P9 is P5’s mother. Fact 15: P5 is P9’s daughter. Fact 16: P9 is P7’s mother. Fact 20: P7 is P9’s son. Fact 22: P9 is P8’s mother. So P9’s children are P3 (son), P5 (daughter), P7 (son), P8 (son). Wait, that’s a lot. So P9 has four children: P3, P5, P7, P8. But how? Because P9’s parents are P0 and P1. And P9’s spouse must be P2, since P2 is the father of P3, P5, P7, P8. Because P3’s father is P2 (fact 5,19), P5’s father is P2 (fact 2,21), P7’s father is P2 (10,14), P8’s father is P2 (4,23). So P2 and P9 are spouses, parents of these four children.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.15.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">So P2 and P9 are a couple, with children P3, P5, P7, P8. P3 is male, P5 female, P7 male, P8 male.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.16.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">Now, P0 and P1 are parents of P4 (son) and P9 (daughter). So P9 is the daughter of P0 and P1, and married to P2, having four children. P2’s other children? Wait, no, P2’s children are with P9. Wait, but P2’s children are P3, P5, P7, P8 (all with P9), and possibly others? Let me check the facts again.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.17" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.17.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">Wait, the given facts mention that P2 is the father of P5, P3, P7, P8. All these have P9 as their mother. So P2 and P9 are the parents.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.18" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.18.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">So the family structure so far:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.19" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.19.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">- P0 (male) and P1 (female) have children P4 (male) and P9 (female).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.20" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.20.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">- P9 (female) and P2 (male) are married and have children: P3 (male), P5 (female), P7 (male), P8 (male).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.21" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.21.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">Additionally, let’s check if there are other parents or children mentioned.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.22" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.22.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">Looking at fact 24: P1 is P4’s mother. Which we already have.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.23" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.23.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">So now, we need to find grandsons. A grandson of Pi is a son of Pi’s child. So for each person Pi, we need to look at their children, then check if those children have sons, and those sons would be Pi’s grandsons.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.24" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.24.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">So the steps would be:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.25" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.25.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">1. For each Pi, find their children (sons and daughters).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.26" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.26.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">2. For each child of Pi, check if that child has any sons (because a grandson is a son of one’s child).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.27" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.27.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">So for each Pi, collect all sons of their children (both sons and daughters, because a daughter’s son is also a grandson).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.28" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.28.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">Wait, the question is to determine grandsons. So the grandson is the son of one’s child, regardless of the gender of the child. So if Pi has a daughter who has a son, that son is Pi’s grandson.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.29" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.29.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Now, let’s check each person:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.30" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.30.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Starting with P0:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.31" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.31.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Children: P4 (son) and P9 (daughter).</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.32" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.32.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">P4’s children: Are there any? Looking at the given facts, P4’s relations: he is the son of P0 and P1. Are there any mentions of P4 having children? Let me check all facts again. The facts given don’t mention P4 having any children. So P4 has no sons mentioned. So P0’s grandsons would be the sons of P9. P9’s sons are P3, P7, P8. So those three are grandsons of P0.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.33" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.33.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Next, P1:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.34" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.34.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Children: P4 and P9. Again, P4 has no children mentioned, so P1’s grandsons would be P9’s sons: P3, P7, P8.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.35" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.35.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">…<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;" mathcolor="#008080">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>Content omitted for more compact presentation<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;" mathcolor="#008080">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>…</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.36" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.36.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">P9:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.37" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.37.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Her children are P3, P5, P7, P8. None of them have children mentioned. So P9 has no grandsons.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.38" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.38.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Wait, but wait. Let’s confirm again. The facts given only mention the relations provided. If a person isn’t mentioned as a parent, we assume they don’t have children in this family tree. So unless stated, we can’t assume any.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.39" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.39.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Therefore, for each Pi, their grandsons are the sons of their children, but only if those children have sons as per the given data.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.40" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.40.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">So let’s go through each Pi:</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.41" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.41.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">P0: Children P4 and P9.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.42" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.42.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">- P4 has no children mentioned.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.43" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.43.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">- P9 has sons P3, P7, P8.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.44" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.44.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">So P0’s grandsons: P3, P7, P8.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.45" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.45.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">P1: Children P4 and P9.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.46" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.46.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Same as P0, grandsons P3, P7, P8.</span></span>
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.47" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.2.47.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">…<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&lt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>Content omitted for more compact presentation<math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.pic1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">&gt;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;</annotation></semantics></math>…</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summarization and Comprehension</span> (<span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">Red</span>): DeepSeek-R1 effectively summarizes the relationships in the user prompt, listing them in a clear and coherent manner. This demonstrates foundational capabilities in information extraction and synthesis.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Abstraction and Filtering</span> (<span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#0000FF;">Blue</span>): DeepSeek-R1 identifies potentially redundant or conflicting information and cross-examines each individual. It also deduces intermediate relations, such as parent and child, before addressing the target grandson relation. This reflects structured reasoning with abstraction.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Verification and Confirmation</span> (<span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">Brown</span>, <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">Teal</span>): DeepSeek-R1 repeatedly verifies and validates intermediate deductions. While this process may appear somewhat unstructured, it mimics a natural human reasoning approach, and contributes to robust inference. For example, DeepSeek-R1 verifies and confirms its findings on parent and child relations repeatedly before proceeding to deduce the grandson relation in the part highlighted in <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#BF8040;">brown</span> color. Also, it evaluates each person’s potential grandsons, repeating the verification process multiple times before finalizing its conclusions in the part highlighted in <span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#008080;">teal</span> color.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Planning</span> (<span id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#800080;">Violet</span>): DeepSeek-R1 articulates a plan to deduce the grandson relation, though interestingly, this plan emerges midway through the reasoning process rather than at the outset. This raises intriguing questions about the internal reasoning loop and how DeepSeek-R1 dynamically constructs strategies during inference.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In detail, this phenomenon demonstrates the competent planning ability inside the deep reasoning capabilities of DeepSeek-R1, a particularly important ability for handling complicated reasoning problems which can hardly be solved in a short period of time and reasoning process. However, this raises the question why such plan of checking grandson relation suddenly pops up in the middle of the reasoning process rather than in the beginning of the reasoning process. In particular, it draws ones to ask how DeepSeek-R1 knows that it should deduce intermediate relations like parent and child before addressing the relation grandson if such plan is only derived afterwards. While DeepSeek-R1 demonstrates strong planning ability, the reasoning process may not be entirely well-organized and structured, suggesting that, despite appearing coherent, the underlying reasoning rationale may not always be fully sound and deserve further investigation.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, DeepSeek-R1 demonstrates a unique blend of planning, structured reasoning, iterative validation, and robust abstraction, all of which are hallmarks of advanced deep reasoning. However, due to token limitations, this process becomes infeasible at <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math>, where reasoning depth and prompt length scale significantly.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Despite architectural limitations imposed by token constraints, DeepSeek-R1 consistently demonstrates superior performance in deep relational reasoning across both family tree and general graph reasoning benchmarks. Its long CoT paradigm enables it to engage in rich, human-like reasoning processes that remain unmatched by current models without dedicated deep reasoning mechanisms. These findings highlight both the promise and limitations of current LLMs in advancing toward more sophisticated cognitive capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Future Directions</span></h3>

<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal Reasoning</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our findings reaffirm that deep reasoning capabilities are critical for tackling relational reasoning tasks. However, recent studies have shown that LLMs may exhibit greater proficiency in multimodal reasoning in domains such as time-series analysis than in purely text-based reasoning tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. Multimodal reasoning involves processing and integrating information from non-textual sources such as images or videos, enabling spatial, geometric, and visual cognition. Given that humans naturally rely on spatial and visual reasoning for a wide range of everyday tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>, an important future direction is to explore whether relational reasoning in LLMs can be further enhanced through multimodal prompts. This raises the question: could visual or diagrammatic representations of relational structures, for example, family trees or graphs, serve as a more effective input modality for enabling and strengthening deep relational reasoning?</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Deeper Investigation into Long Chain-of-Thought Processes</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in our analysis of DeepSee–R1’s reasoning trace, LLMs equipped with deep reasoning capabilities can engage in remarkably thorough and robust inference prior to generating a final answer. However, as noted in the planning phase of the reasoning process, DeepSeek-R1 occasionally constructs its plan midway through reasoning rather than at the outset. This observation raises important concerns regarding the coherence and soundness of the internal reasoning rationale. While the output may appear logically consistent, the underlying reasoning process may, at times, lack proper structure or contain subtle flaws.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This opens up a rich line of inquiry into the internal reasoning dynamics of such models. A promising future direction involves conducting fine-grained analysis of reasoning traces that ultimately lead to incorrect conclusions. By scrutinizing these examples, we may uncover latent error patterns, such as premature assumptions, flawed inferences, or inconsistencies in intermediate steps, that can inform the design of novel training objectives, architectural modifications, or reasoning frameworks. Such efforts could significantly enhance the robustness and interpretability of deep reasoning in LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Conclusion</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we systematically evaluated the deep relational reasoning capabilities of state-of-the-art LLMs through a series of benchmark problems in family tree and general graph reasoning. Among the three models evaluated, DeepSeek-R1, DeepSeek-V3 and GPT-4o, our experimental results consistently demonstrate the superior performance of DeepSeek-R1, especially at smaller problem scales (<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> and 20). Its ability to perform accurate and structured logical deductions across multiple layers of relational dependencies confirms its potential for deep reasoning tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, our analysis also reveals key limitations. All models, including DeepSeek-R1, exhibit performance degradation at larger problem sizes (<math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=40</annotation></semantics></math>), primarily due to intrinsic token limits and incomplete output generations. A detailed investigation into DeepSeek-R1’s reasoning traces shows that, while its long Chain-of-Thought processes are a key strength, they are not always well-structured or logically sound throughout, raising important questions about the underlying reasoning mechanisms of such models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Looking ahead, we identify two promising directions for future work. First, the potential of multimodal reasoning, incorporating visual and spatial inputs, may further enhance the relational reasoning capabilities of LLMs. Second, a deeper examination of long Chain-of-Thought responses, especially those that lead to incorrect outcomes, can offer insights into the cognitive patterns of LLMs and guide improvements in both training and inference strategies.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our study highlights both the promise and current limitations of LLMs in performing deep relational reasoning. It provides an empirical and analytical foundation for future research aimed at building more robust, interpretable, and human-aligned reasoning systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
